d and so much so  that that  emanuel kant the great you know german  philosopher  thought that causality was one of the  basic categories through which we  we understand the world david hume the  person who's rea  he's reacting to said that we can't  actually observe  cause and effect but also thought we  couldn't get away from it and there's  much more that could be said  about all the ins and outs of the  history of of this this concept  causality provides us with  answers to the question why why are  things the way they are  why did they turn out the way they did  why did this happen why didn't this  happen  why am i getting sick why did somebody  have to die  why can't we fix what's going on here  those are all things that can be  answered by thinking about causality and  as we explore the causality of things  we obtain a certain intelligibility we  another way of talking about that is we  understand things better  as we explore and investigate causal  connections and  matrices and  real things the stuff that we experience  in our our life the things that we are  going to be talking about  uh forever and acting upon are  typically complex themselves and they're  caught up within  complex causal matrices in which  there isn't a nice simple picture of  things  there are some things you know i can  turn on a light  and you know the the electricity flows  to it and  the light goes on and now i see it and  the room is illuminated  but most things don't work in quite such  a simple  way at the same time we human beings  have a tendency to want  rather simple causal explanations for  things and to prefer them and to talk  about them  quite a bit i should also point out too  that we human beings  complicate things quite a bit because  we are also causal agents  and we not only act we interpret  causality and make decisions  on what we think to be its basis and and  advise each other we engage in  deliberation so on the one hand we have  simplicity is something that we often  desire  sometimes to our detriment and the  detriment of others on the other hand we  have complexity  the contrast that i want to talk about  here  in terms of complex  and simplistic causality is not the same  thing as  simple and complex causality simple  causality would be there's some sort of  causal process and it can actually be  described in simple terms because it  actually is simple and  the vast causal networks that we're part  of are composed of  much smaller simpler parts which may in  fact  connect with each other but could be  isolated out from each other as well  if not we could never fix anything  but we also  have our explanations and our  interpretations of things  those can be simplistic  that is too simple being presented  in a way that doesn't do adequate  justice to what it is that we're trying  to  investigate so you know frequently the  things that we  are experiencing and discussing and  deliberating and choosing and acting on  they involve complex interactions and  connections so this tendency  towards over simplification is  a a problem precisely because in doing  so we are making things  too simple i'm not saying by the way  that we always need to understand  causal nexuses and connections and their  absolute fullness in order to you know  understand everything this is sort of a  relative distinction  so when we're thinking for example in  in this time of covid19 about people who  are talking about the need to reopen the  economy  why because reopening the economy will  prevent a currently existing harm which  is people being out of work and not  being able to get food and eat  that's an example of over simplistic  understandings  of causal factors and matrices  the economy by itself i mean it wasn't  actually closed if it was then then we  wouldn't have any economic activity  going on  right but the economy is an incredibly  vast thing with all of these different  things going on within it  and what we're talking about doing you  